# CoW-charter precision (feedback-cow-charter-optimal-clones): a USER `get`
# (a method on a user struct, NOT a builtin collection) returns an OWNED temp —
# its root is the temp, not the receiver. The projection-root descent is gated
# on the receiver's builtin collection-KIND ∈ {Array, OrderedMap}, so a user
# `get` is NOT descended: NO materialize of `b`, hence NO `<UserStruct>` clone at
# the write site (a clone here would be a wasted-clone == charter breach).
# The write lands on the throwaway temp, so the caller is UNCHANGED (prints 1).
# ⚠ KNOWN DIVERGENCE (output-review 2026-07-21, filed): Rust gg SILENTLY ACCEPTS
# this write-to-an-rvalue-place and DISCARDS it (prints 1, no warning); ggdef
# REJECTS it ("expression is not a place"). This fixture pins Rust's CURRENT
# behavior (Track C only guarantees NO wasted clone). The reference-grade fix =
# Rust should reject/warn to match ggdef — SAME class as the R-LVALUE residual
# (assign to a field of an rvalue-returned place); the recursive-lvalue-gate
# follow-up covers it. Rust is the lagging lane here.
# The companion `getchain_user_get_no_clone` clones-report assertion
# (tests/integration.rs) pins "0 implicit clones".
struct R:
    int id

struct Bag:
    Vector[R] backing

equip Bag:
    R get(self, int i):
        return R(self.backing.get(i).unwrap().id)

void poke(Bag b):
    b.get(0).id = 9

void main():
    Bag a = Bag([R(1)])
    poke(a)
    print(a.backing.get(0).unwrap().id)   # 1 (write hit a temp, not `a`)
